  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nWildfire smoke will continue to move in today. While most of this \nsmoke will stay above the surface, portions of northeastern SD could \nsee some of this smoke get to the surface. The concentration of \nsmoke will not be enough to cause a decrease in visibilities, though \nthe air quality will drop into the moderate Air Quality Index \ncategory. Those who are sensitive to air pollutants could be \nimpacted slightly. Temperatures today are on track to be around \nnormal, with highs in the 80s.\n\nAs a weak upper-level shortwave moves over SD this afternoon into \nthe evening, a surface boundary will be moving into central and \nnortheastern SD. This shortwave and surface boundary will help to \nprovide some forcing for shower and storm development today as they \nmove over an area with some instability. The models are showing CAPE \nvalues of 1000-2000 J/kg over central and portions of northeast SD \nthis afternoon into the evening, decreasing as the surface boundary \nmoves in. Mid-level flow from the intermountain west will help to \nmove in moisture in the mid-levels and create a bit of an inverted-V \nsounding. The inverted-V sounding indicates 2 things: that the \nconvection will likely be elevated, and that strong winds from \nmicrobursts will be possible in the storms. There is a slight (level \n2 of 5) to marginal (level 1 of 5) risk that any storms that \ndevelops could become severe, with the highest risk for severe \nstorms over south central SD. The main hazard will be strong wind \ngusts of 60-70 mph. Even though there will be weaker lapse rates, if \nany isolated cell does develop in front of the boundary, there is a \nrisk for hail up around 1 inch. Additionally, with the moisture \nmoving in from the intermountain west helping to feed the storms, \nthere could be heavy rainfall at times. If multiple cells were to \nmove over the same area, that rainfall could add up and create \nlocalized flooding at times. These storms should move out of central \nand northeastern SD during the early overnight hours, with the \npotential for a few lingering showers into early Thursday morning. \n\nBehind the surface boundary, cooler air will be moving in Thursday. \nThis will cause temperatures to fall slightly, to be about 5 to 10 \ndegrees below normal through the weekend. High temperatures will be \nin the 70s during this time. There is a little bit of variability in \nthe models as to precipitation chances Thursday. Some models keep \nsome isolated showers and storms over central and northeastern SD \nThursday morning and afternoon, while other models keep the showers \nand storms out of the area. The next larger chance for showers and \nstorms to move in will be Thursday night into Friday as the Lee of \nthe Rockies surface trough starts to move over and through SD. \nModels show very weak instability over northeastern and central SD \nas well as weak lapse rates and shear. Because of this, these \nshowers and storms will likely stay as weak storms with little \nchance to become severe.",
